{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"03","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"ETHOBOT CAT100 Orientation","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting ethobot cat100 orientation to AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Bring a draft of your own to the tutor instead of asking it to generate from scratch.","Record each tutor suggestion as accepted, modified, or rejected with a one-line reason.","Keep rejected suggestions in the log so the trail shows what you turned down.","Use the tutor to sharpen claims without letting it invent facts or inflate them."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/02"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_calibration","cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_modify_path"]},"content":"# ETHOBOT CAT100 Orientation\n\n![ETHOBOT CAT100 Orientation textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-03-ethobot-cat100-orientation.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nETHOBOT is the AI tutor you will use throughout CAT 100. The point of meeting it now is to set the relationship up correctly: ETHOBOT is a critic, not an author. It can react to a draft you wrote, point out weak spots, and propose changes, but the deciding vote is always yours. A student who treats the tutor as an oracle copies whatever it says; a student who treats it as a reviewer keeps a record of which suggestions they took, changed, or refused.\n\nThe work product for this orientation is an AI critique log that shows accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions. The log is the proof that you ran the tutor, not the other way around. Every row records a suggestion and your call on it, so an instructor can see your judgment rather than just the final text.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Bring a draft of your own to the tutor instead of asking it to generate from scratch.\n- Record each tutor suggestion as accepted, modified, or rejected with a one-line reason.\n- Keep rejected suggestions in the log so the trail shows what you turned down.\n- Use the tutor to sharpen claims without letting it invent facts or inflate them.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nDevon asks ETHOBOT to review one resume bullet: \"Helped with the club's fall event and boosted attendance.\" The tutor returns three suggestions: add a number, replace \"helped\" with \"led,\" and add \"expert event planner.\" A passive user would accept all three and submit \"Led the fall event as an expert event planner, boosting attendance.\" That sentence is now partly false: Devon co-led, ran one event, and is not an expert.\n\nThe orientation skill is to take each suggestion separately. One is a clear improvement, one needs a truthful tweak, and one must be refused. The log is where those three different calls become visible.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA critique log treats every AI suggestion as a proposal that must clear a bar before it enters your work. There are exactly three outcomes, and a strong log uses all three.\n\n| Decision | Meaning | When to use it |\n|----------|---------|----------------|\n| Accepted | You take the suggestion as-is | It improves the work and stays truthful |\n| Modified | You take part of the idea, change the rest | The direction is right but the wording overreaches |\n| Rejected | You decline it | It is false, off-target, or weakens the claim |\n\nThe hardest and most important column is **rejected**. A log with only \"accepted\" rows is indistinguishable from copy-paste; it shows no judgment. The rejected and modified rows are the evidence that a person sat between the tool and the page. This is why the orientation insists you record the rejections: the value you add as a learner lives in the suggestions you did not take. Research on AI-assisted decision-making backs this discipline: people frequently accept an AI's suggestion even when it is wrong, and interventions that force deliberate engagement, exactly what a reject-or-modify row demands, measurably reduce that overreliance (Bucinca, Malaya, & Gajos, 2021).\n\n**AI-use disclosure in 2026.** ETHOBOT is the course's bounded tutor, but the same accept/modify/reject discipline applies to the general-purpose assistants you will also encounter, such as ChatGPT, Claude, or Microsoft Copilot. As of 2026, many programs require an AI-use disclosure that names which tool was used, for what purpose, and to what extent, and your critique log doubles as exactly that disclosure: it already records the tool, the suggestions, and your calls on them. Keeping the log current here means the disclosure you assemble later in the course is already written.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reviewer opens a critique log and every row says &quot;Accepted: good idea.&quot; What does that pattern tell them?\" explanation=\"A log with only accepted rows is indistinguishable from copy-paste. The modify and reject rows, with specific reasons, are what show a person evaluated the tutor instead of obeying it.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The learner exercised strong judgment\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The log shows no judgment and reads like the learner accepted everything\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The tutor must be perfectly accurate\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The rejected rows were correctly deleted to keep it clean\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft of a log often hides the real decision behind a vague summary:\n\n> ETHOBOT helped me improve my bullet.\n\nThat sentence is too thin because it does not name the suggestions or your calls on them. A reviewer cannot tell whether you exercised judgment or simply accepted everything. A strong log shows each suggestion and your decision, including the one you refused.\n\n### Worked Example: turning a tutor conversation into a critique log\n\nYou ask ETHOBOT to review one resume bullet and it returns three suggestions. Build the log row by row.\n\n| # | Tutor suggestion | Your decision | Reason |\n|---|------------------|---------------|--------|\n| 1 | Add a number to \"boosted attendance\" | Accepted | Changed to \"raised event attendance from 15 to 40\" |\n| 2 | Replace \"helped\" with \"led\" | Modified | I co-led, so I used \"co-led a 4-person team\" to stay truthful |\n| 3 | Add \"expert event planner\" | Rejected | One event does not make me an expert; the claim is not defensible |\n\nFinal bullet: `Co-led a 4-person team that raised event attendance from 15 to 40 in one month.`\n\nThe log records all three suggestions, not just the ones you used. A reader can see the accepted, modified, and rejected calls and the reason for each.\n\nWork Devon's three tutor suggestions as a decision tree. For each one, pick accept, modify, or reject before you read the feedback.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Routing ETHOBOT three suggestions on the event bullet\",\"start\":\"s1\",\"nodes\":{\"s1\":{\"prompt\":\"Suggestion 1: Add a number to \\\"boosted attendance.\\\" You can verify attendance rose from 15 to 40. What do you log?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept and add the verifiable number\",\"next\":\"s2\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The number is true and makes the claim concrete, so it enters the work as-is.\"},{\"label\":\"Reject it\",\"next\":\"s2\",\"feedback\":\"That throws away a truthful improvement. A verifiable number belongs in the bullet, so accept is the better call.\"}]},\"s2\":{\"prompt\":\"Suggestion 2: Replace \\\"helped\\\" with \\\"led.\\\" You actually co-led a 4-person team. What do you log?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept \\\"led\\\" as written\",\"next\":\"s3\",\"feedback\":\"That overstates your role. \\\"Led\\\" implies sole leadership, so this needs a truthful tweak, not a straight accept.\"},{\"label\":\"Modify to \\\"co-led a 4-person team\\\"\",\"next\":\"s3\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The direction is right but the wording overreached, so you trim it down to what you can defend.\"}]},\"s3\":{\"prompt\":\"Suggestion 3: Add \\\"expert event planner.\\\" You have run one event. What do you log?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept it: it sounds impressive\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"One event does not make you an expert. An impressive but false label is a downgrade to your credibility, so this should be rejected.\"},{\"label\":\"Reject and log why: one event does not support \\\"expert\\\"\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. You reject it and keep the rejection visible in the log as proof you guarded the truth.\"}]},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"Final bullet: \\\"Co-led a 4-person team that raised event attendance from 15 to 40 in one month.\\\" The log shows one accept, one modify, one reject.\",\"feedback\":\"A log that uses all three decision types is the evidence that a person sat between the tool and the page.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nNow order the loop you just ran so it becomes a reusable habit.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Bring your own draft to the tutor\",\"Capture every suggestion the tutor returns\",\"Check each suggestion for improvement and for truth\",\"Decide accept, modify, or reject with a one-line reason\",\"Keep the rejected rows visible in the log\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor every suggestion the tutor makes, ask whether it makes the claim both stronger and still true. If yes, accept it. If the idea is good but the wording overreaches, modify it down to what you can defend. If it is false, irrelevant, or inflates your role, reject it and log why. The rule that keeps you safe is simple: the tutor may improve how you say something, but you decide what is true.\n\n1. **Improvement check:** Does the suggestion make the claim clearer or stronger?\n2. **Truth check:** Is the suggested wording still something you could defend?\n3. **Logging check:** Did you record the decision and reason, including for rejections?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is accepting a suggestion because it sounds more impressive. \"Expert,\" \"spearheaded,\" and \"led\" feel like upgrades, but if they describe work you did not do, they are downgrades to your credibility. The tutor optimizes for polish; you have to optimize for truth.\n\nA second misreading is deleting the rejected suggestions from the log to make it look clean. That removes exactly the evidence that proves you were in control. A log with visible rejections is more trustworthy than a tidy log that shows only accepted changes.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nSuppose you bring the tutor a short \"About me\" paragraph for a class profile. ETHOBOT suggests five changes. Rather than reading them as a to-do list, open a three-column table and route each one: a tighter opening sentence (accept), a claim that you \"mentored dozens of students\" when you mentored three (modify to \"mentored 3 first-year students\"), a suggestion to add a hobby that is not true (reject), a grammar fix (accept), and a recommendation to add a metric you cannot verify (reject). In ten minutes you have a profile that reads well and a log that shows five decisions.\n\nThe walkthrough's lesson is that the tutor's output is raw material, not a verdict. The orientation is teaching you a stance you will reuse in every later module: read AI output as a set of proposals, sort them, and keep the sort. When you reach the verification and accessibility sections, you will be running the same loop on facts and on design, but the habit of logging accepted, modified, and rejected starts here.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you read someone else's critique log, look for whether the three decision types are all present and whether the reasons are specific. A log that says \"accepted: good idea\" for every row is weak; a log that says \"rejected: I never used Python, so I cannot claim it\" shows a person guarding the truth. The reasons are where the learning shows.\n\nA strong reader of a critique log can answer four questions:\n\n1. Did the learner bring their own draft, or did the tool generate it?\n2. Are there modified and rejected rows, not just accepted ones?\n3. Does each reason name a real reason (truth, fit, scope), not just \"better\"?\n4. Is the final artifact consistent with the decisions the log records?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour critique log is strong when a reviewer can reconstruct your reasoning from the log alone, without re-running the tutor. The earlier layers are bringing your own draft, capturing every suggestion, and recording an honest decision for each; the last layer is tidiness. If a suggestion is tempting but you are unsure, mark it in the log and ask BigAL to compare the truthful version against the inflated version rather than asking it to decide for you.\n\nA useful note would read: \"ETHOBOT gave three suggestions on my event bullet. I accepted the number, modified 'led' to 'co-led' because that is accurate, and rejected 'expert event planner' because one event does not support that claim. The final bullet matches what I actually did.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the critique-log habit from a resume bullet to a piece of code the tutor reviews. The structure is identical: ETHOBOT might suggest renaming a variable (accept), wrapping a block in a try/except (modify, because you only want to catch one specific error), and \"rewriting the whole function to be cleaner\" (reject, because the assignment grades your logic). The decision types do not change when the medium changes; what changes is the truth and ownership test you apply. A code rewrite that hides your logic is the same kind of overreach as an \"expert\" label you did not earn. If you have internalized the accept/modify/reject loop here, you can run it on writing, on data, on design, and on code without learning a new skill each time.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-03-ethobot-cat100-orientation-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI critique log showing accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show the draft you brought to the tutor. The revised version should show the final artifact plus the log of accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions. The trace should explain why at least one suggestion was rejected.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a copy-everything log with a log that includes a real rejection.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the accept/modify/reject loop to a new kind of artifact.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes one of your decisions.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are building a critique log for CAT 100 01.03. A peer comment notes that every row is marked accepted, so the log shows no judgment. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show you evaluated the tutor's suggestions.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation\", \"ai_supported_learning\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your critique log records each tutor suggestion, your decision (accepted, modified, or rejected), and a specific reason, and that the rejected rows are still visible.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Bucinca, Z., Malaya, P., & Gajos, K. Z. (2021). To trust or to think: Cognitive forcing reduces overreliance on AI in AI-assisted decision-making. Proc. ACM HCI (CSCW). https://doi.org/10.1145/3449287\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"You finish a draft and ask ETHOBOT, \"Did I pass?\" Why is this the wrong question for the tutor?","options":["ETHOBOT only answers questions typed in all capital letters.","ETHOBOT is a practice and feedback tutor; your instructor and the rubric assign the official grade, so ask for feedback instead.","ETHOBOT can grade but charges for it.","Passing questions must be emailed, not typed."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The tutor helps you prepare and revise; it does not set the official grade. Ask for actionable feedback, not a verdict.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_tutor_grades","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ethobot_cat100_orientation"],"stem":"Which request gives ETHOBOT enough to give you useful, specific feedback?","options":["help","Here is my resume bullet: \"Helped with events.\" Does it have an action verb and a measurable result, and how would you tighten it?","is this good?","fix it"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A useful request states the goal, includes the artifact, and names what you want checked. Vague prompts get vague answers.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_vague_request","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"Which message keeps your use of ETHOBOT inside its intended role as a tutor?","options":["Write my whole discussion post and I will paste it in.","Point out two places where my discussion post lacks supporting evidence so I can fix them myself.","Submit this post to the gradebook for me.","Tell me the exact grade I will receive."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Asking for targeted feedback you then act on keeps the work and judgment yours. Asking the tutor to author or submit it does not.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_answer_machine","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"ETHOBOT gives you three suggestions. You apply two and ignore one because it would make a false claim. What does a complete critique log record?","options":["Only the two changes you made.","All three: the two you accepted (with what you changed) and the one you rejected, with a one-line reason for each.","Just a note that you talked to the tutor.","Only the suggestion you rejected, since that was the hard call."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A complete log shows accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions with reasons, so a reader can audit your judgment.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_log_skips_rejections","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_modify_path","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","stem":"The tutor suggests replacing your resume line 'helped organize the volunteer program' with 'managed the entire volunteer program.' You coordinated one of its three committees. Which log entry is right?","options":["Accept: the tutor's version is stronger and recruiters like strong verbs.","Reject: the suggestion touched your wording, so the whole idea is unusable.","Modify: keep the stronger verb but trim to the defensible scope, e.g. 'coordinated one of three committees in the volunteer program,' and log what you changed and why.","Accept now, and plan to soften it if anyone asks."],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The suggestion is directionally right (the original undersells) but overreaches (you did not manage the entire program). The modify move keeps the improvement while keeping the claim defensible, and the log records the trim so a reviewer can see the judgment, not just the outcome.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_accept_or_reject_only","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_tutor_grades","pattern":"tutor_assumed_to_grade","description":"Believing the ETHOBOT tutor assigns the official course grade or replaces the instructor","trigger":"learner expects the tutor's response to be the final graded score","feedback":"ETHOBOT is a practice and feedback tutor, not the grader. Your instructor and the rubric set the official grade; use the tutor to prepare.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_vague_request","pattern":"vague_help_request","description":"Asking the tutor a vague question like 'help' or 'is this good' with no goal or context","trigger":"learner sends a one-word or contextless request to the tutor","feedback":"Give the tutor a goal, the artifact, and what you are stuck on, for example: 'Check whether this resume bullet has a measurable result.'","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_answer_machine","pattern":"tutor_as_answer_machine","description":"Asking the tutor to produce the finished assignment instead of feedback you can act on","trigger":"learner asks the tutor to write or complete the deliverable","feedback":"Ask for feedback, a comparison, or a next step you can apply. The work and the judgment stay yours.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_ethobot_cat100_orientation_log_skips_rejections","pattern":"critique_log_omits_rejected","description":"Recording only accepted tutor suggestions in the critique log and dropping the rejected ones","trigger":"learner logs only changes they made and not suggestions they declined","feedback":"A complete critique log records accepted, modified, and rejected suggestions, each with a one-line reason.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_3_accept_or_reject_only","pattern":"accept_or_reject_only","description":"Treating every tutor or AI suggestion as a binary accept-or-reject and skipping the modify path that trims an overreaching suggestion down to a defensible claim.","trigger":"learner logs only accepted and rejected suggestions and never records a modified one","feedback":"Many suggestions are directionally right but overreach. The third move is modify: keep the direction, trim the claim to what you can defend, and log the change. A log with no modified rows usually means overreaching suggestions were swallowed whole or discarded wholesale.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"197c6b9cff1b0e3aced70f30c1b8c826cc9bc129db733c2d9e272f612473703f","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}